<ContentLayout title="Free Website Audit for Small Businesses: A Practical Workflow" description="Review your key business pages with a free HTML check, Search Console and manual customer-journey checks. Record evidence and choose the next fix." breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} toc={toc} publishDate="2026-03-28" updateDate="2026-09-11" showCTA={false}>
<p>A free small-business website audit combines a review of important pages with checks of the tasks customers need to complete. Start with your homepage, main service or product page and contact or booking journey. Record a problem only when you can show the evidence and explain the expected behaviour.</p>
<p>AuditWeb's <a href="/free-website-audit/">free HTML checker</a> covers pasted page markup. It does not crawl your website, measure page speed, test forms or inspect your Google Business Profile. The workflow below explains where those separate checks fit.</p>

<h2 id="why-small-businesses-need-audits">How should a small business choose its audit scope?</h2>
<p>Choose pages by business importance and the problem that triggered the review. A missed enquiry needs a different investigation from a decline in search impressions.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Customers cannot contact you:</strong> inspect the contact details, form validation, delivery configuration and confirmation message.</li>
  <li><strong>Search traffic changed:</strong> compare completed Search Console periods by page and query before choosing a content fix.</li>
  <li><strong>A redesign is planned:</strong> inventory valuable URLs, their purpose and existing links before changing navigation or addresses.</li>
  <li><strong>The site is hard to use on a phone:</strong> test navigation, text, buttons and the customer journey at narrow widths.</li>
</ul>
<p>Write the included URLs, access available and review date. A small, reproducible sample is more useful than claiming that an untested whole site has passed.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-check">What does the free HTML checker inspect?</h2>
<p>The checker reports title and description markup, canonical declarations, HTML indexing directives, H1 elements, image alt attributes, viewport metadata and a social preview image. Its observations describe the source you supply.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Open a public page and copy its page source.</li>
  <li>Enter the page URL for relative canonical resolution and paste the HTML.</li>
  <li>Run the check and compare flagged observations with the intended page behaviour.</li>
  <li>Download the Markdown findings and record the affected URL.</li>
</ol>
<p>A <code>noindex</code> directive may be deliberate. An empty alt attribute can be correct for a decorative image. A missing canonical does not prove a Google indexing problem. The <a href="/free-website-audit/seo/">SEO HTML interpretation guide</a> explains these distinctions.</p>

<h2 id="common-small-business-issues">Which checks need evidence beyond page source?</h2>
<p>Search visibility, live performance and working customer journeys need their own evidence sources.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Google indexing:</strong> use <a href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/9012289">URL Inspection</a> for the indexed and live views of an important page. A successful live test does not guarantee inclusion.</li>
  <li><strong>Page speed:</strong> use <a href="https://pagespeed.web.dev/">PageSpeed Insights</a> and distinguish field experience from a single lab run. Record the tested URL and device.</li>
  <li><strong>Business information:</strong> verify the public name, contact details, opening information and actual service area. Google's <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/3038177">Business Profile guidelines</a> explain eligibility and representation requirements.</li>
  <li><strong>Enquiries:</strong> arrange a clearly labelled test with the person receiving submissions. Check validation, the confirmation state and receipt; a success message alone does not prove delivery.</li>
  <li><strong>Accessibility:</strong> navigate with a keyboard and inspect labels, focus and text at zoom. Automated reports cover only part of the review.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use test accounts and an agreed procedure for booking or payment flows. Avoid creating real orders or misleading customer records merely to test an interface.</p>

<h2 id="quick-wins">How should you prioritise confirmed problems?</h2>
<p>Prioritise a blocked customer task or unintended access restriction before polishing otherwise usable pages. Then compare the affected scope, confidence and effort of the remaining work.</p>
<p>A broken contact route has a clear retest: the agreed test enquiry reaches its destination. An unclear service title has a different retest: the released title accurately describes the page. Whether that edit changes search clicks requires a later comparable measurement.</p>
<p>Do not treat a generic character count, word count or tool score as a universal pass condition. For title changes, use Google's <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/title-link">descriptive title guidance</a> and keep the page's actual service and audience clear.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-invest-in-a-full-audit">When is a professional review useful?</h2>
<p>A professional review is useful when the question needs evidence or expertise beyond your access: a complex migration, a persistent search loss, a checkout problem, an accessibility assessment or a security investigation.</p>
<p>Ask for the proposed URL sample, systems included, deliverables, exclusions and verification method. A price or package label does not establish what will be tested. Review our <a href="/website-audit-services/">service scope</a> and <a href="/website-audit-services/report-example/">completed owned-site report example</a> before deciding what evidence you need.</p>

<h2 id="getting-started">What should your first audit record contain?</h2>
<p>Record the URL, observation date, evidence, consequence, proposed correction, owner and retest. Mark unavailable checks as untested rather than passed.</p>
<p>The <a href="/resources/agency-audit-toolkit/">editable agency toolkit</a> supplies blank working files. The separate <a href="/website-audit-checklist/template/">50-check PDF</a> supports a broader manual review. Neither file automatically scans or scores your site.</p>
